import{_ as t,o,c as a,ao as d}from"./chunks/framework.c8yPS5zV.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Operation contracts","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"operations.md","filePath":"operations.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"operations.md"};function i(r,e,c,s,l,p){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="Operation-contracts" tabindex="-1">Operation contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#Operation-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Operation contracts {#Operation-contracts}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page describes the Julia interface. An operation in tenferro-rs does not automatically imply the same operation is exposed by Tenferro.jl.</p><h2 id="Types-and-devices" tabindex="-1">Types and devices <a class="header-anchor" href="#Types-and-devices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Types and devices {#Types-and-devices}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>AutoTenferro()</code> and <code>AutoTenferroTraced()</code> accept CPU arrays of <code>Float32</code>, <code>Float64</code>, <code>ComplexF32</code>, and <code>ComplexF64</code>. Integer and <code>Bool</code> arrays are storage and forward-computation types, not differentiable inputs. GPU construction, device transfer, and GPU execution are not exposed by this Julia package. Converting a GPU array through the <code>AbstractArray</code> constructor would copy to the host; it does not select a GPU backend.</p><p><code>TypedTensor</code> provides storage and Julia&#39;s <code>AbstractArray</code> fallback operations. Those fallback operations are not recorded for Tenferro AD. Use <code>EagerTensor</code> or <code>TracedTensor</code> for the Rust operation and AD surfaces described here.</p><h2 id="Arithmetic-and-broadcasting" tabindex="-1">Arithmetic and broadcasting <a class="header-anchor" href="#Arithmetic-and-broadcasting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Arithmetic and broadcasting {#Arithmetic-and-broadcasting}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>A + B</code> and <code>A - B</code> require equal shapes. <code>A * B</code> is matrix multiplication (or a matrix–vector product), whereas <code>A .* B</code> is elementwise multiplication. Dot operations support Julia&#39;s leading-axis broadcast convention: missing trailing dimensions have extent one. The Julia adapter explicitly expands operands before calling Rust, so Rust&#39;s own implicit broadcast convention is not used. Cotangents of expanded axes are summed back to each input&#39;s shape.</p><p>Binary dot operations are <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>rem</code> (also <code>.%</code>), <code>min</code>, and <code>max</code>. Scalars and constant Julia arrays can appear on either side. Mixed floating-point and complex operands use Julia&#39;s <code>promote_type</code>; cotangents are converted back to each active input&#39;s type. Unsupported promoted types are rejected. Tensors in one operation must share a context.</p><p>Same-type <code>Int32</code>/<code>Int64</code> tensors support forward <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, elementwise <code>*</code>, <code>rem</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>abs</code>, and <code>sign</code>. Elementwise <code>/</code> converts integer and Boolean operands to <code>Float64</code>, matching Julia rather than Rust&#39;s integer division. Mixed integer tensor types and integer type promotion are not a supported general interface. Boolean arithmetic is otherwise rejected by Rust.</p><p><code>clamp.(x, lo, hi)</code> is composed as <code>min.(max.(x, lo), hi)</code> and allows broadcast bounds. <code>abs.</code>, <code>sign.</code>, <code>conj.</code>, and unary <code>-</code> are elementwise operations. Complex <code>abs</code> returns the corresponding real element type. Ordering operations (<code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>clamp</code>, <code>minimum</code>, <code>maximum</code>) are restricted to real values.</p><p>Derivatives at quotient discontinuities of <code>rem</code>, zero of <code>abs</code>/<code>sign</code>, power branch points, or equal operands of <code>min</code>/<code>max</code> are not classical derivatives. <code>rem</code> uses derivatives <code>1</code> and <code>-div(x,y)</code> away from its discontinuities; its divisor must be nonzero. Complex <code>sign</code> uses the real derivative of <code>z / abs(z)</code> away from zero. Do not use finite differences across these discontinuities as a gradient reference.</p><h2 id="Elementwise-mathematical-functions" tabindex="-1">Elementwise mathematical functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Elementwise-mathematical-functions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Elementwise mathematical functions {#Elementwise-mathematical-functions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The eight supported broadcast functions call the corresponding Rust operations:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Julia</th><th style="text-align:right;">Derivative away from poles and branch cuts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>exp.(x)</code>, <code>expm1.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>exp.(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>log.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 ./ x</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>log1p.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 ./ (1 .+ x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sqrt.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 ./ (2 .* sqrt.(x))</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sin.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cos.(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cos.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>-sin.(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>tanh.(x)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1 .- tanh.(x).^2</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Complex VJPs conjugate the analytic derivative. Tests compare both precisions and complex types against Julia values, analytic derivatives, and directional finite differences away from singularities. <code>sqrt</code> at zero, <code>log</code> at zero, and <code>log1p</code> at minus one have singular derivatives. Complex branch cuts and poles are excluded from differentiability claims. Real input is not implicitly promoted to complex to extend a function&#39;s domain.</p><p>Real negative arguments of <code>log</code> and <code>sqrt</code>, and arguments below minus one of <code>log1p</code>, currently produce Rust&#39;s IEEE <code>NaN</code>, whereas Julia Base throws a <code>DomainError</code>. <code>log(0)</code> and <code>log1p(-1)</code> produce <code>-Inf</code>. Integer and Boolean inputs to these eight mathematical functions are rejected rather than implicitly converted to floating-point; explicitly convert the input first.</p><p><code>exp(A)</code> is a matrix function and is not provided by these elementwise methods. <code>erf</code> and SpecialFunctions.jl integration are outside the current supported surface; no SpecialFunctions dependency or extension is installed.</p><h2 id="Reductions" tabindex="-1">Reductions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reductions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reductions {#Reductions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>sum</code>, <code>prod</code>, <code>maximum</code>, <code>minimum</code>, and <code>Statistics.mean</code> accept <code>dims</code>. Without <code>dims</code>, they reduce all elements to a scalar. With <code>dims</code>, Julia dimensions are converted from one-based to zero-based Rust axes; reduced dimensions are restored with extent one. Tuples of axes may be unordered. Duplicate or nonpositive axes are rejected; axes beyond the input rank are ignored. An empty tuple of axes returns the input unchanged.</p><p>Product derivatives account for zero factors: one zero receives the product of the other factors, and two or more zeros give zero derivatives. Tied reduction extrema divide the cotangent equally among the tied elements. This is a selected subgradient, not differentiability at a tie. It differs from <a href="https://github.com/JuliaDiff/ChainRules.jl/blob/main/src/rulesets/Base/array.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ChainRules&#39; extrema rules</a>, which route the seed to the single index selected by <code>findmin</code>/<code>findmax</code>. For example, the gradient of <code>minimum([1,1,2])</code> here is <code>[0.5,0.5,0]</code>, whereas ChainRules selects <code>[1,0,0]</code>. Product zero-factor handling agrees with the <a href="https://github.com/JuliaDiff/ChainRules.jl/blob/main/src/rulesets/Base/mapreduce.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ChainRules product rule</a>. These comparisons do not install or invoke ChainRules during Tenferro AD. Complex <code>sum</code>, <code>prod</code>, and <code>mean</code> are supported; complex extrema are not.</p><p>On zero-length reduced axes, the Julia wrapper supplies the sum/product identities, <code>mean</code> returns <code>NaN</code>, and <code>maximum</code>/<code>minimum</code> throw as in Julia. The backend identity extension is tracked in <a href="https://github.com/tensor4all/tenferro-rs/issues/2453" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">tenferro-rs#2453</a>. A zero-length <em>non-reduced</em> dimension gives an empty output with the expected shape. An empty input is distinct from an empty tuple of reduction axes. The dtype and AD guarantees in this section apply to floating/complex types; integer widening (for example Julia&#39;s <code>sum(::Vector{Int32})</code>) and Boolean reductions are not supported Julia-compatible contracts.</p><h2 id="Shapes,-indexing,-and-ownership" tabindex="-1">Shapes, indexing, and ownership <a class="header-anchor" href="#Shapes,-indexing,-and-ownership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shapes, indexing, and ownership {#Shapes,-indexing,-and-ownership}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>reshape</code> follows Julia column-major element order. <code>permutedims</code> accepts a permutation of one-based axes. A vector&#39;s <code>transpose</code> or <code>adjoint</code> has shape <code>(1,n)</code>; a matrix swaps its two axes. <code>adjoint</code> also conjugates values and its pullback conjugates the seed; <code>transpose</code> does not conjugate. Higher-rank <code>transpose</code> is rejected; use <code>permutedims</code> instead.</p><p><code>reverse</code> accepts one dimension or a tuple of dimensions. <code>cat</code> accepts one existing dimension, requires equal extents on every other axis, and promotes floating-point/complex types. New dimensions and block concatenation tuples are not exposed.</p><p>Indexing supports one integer, nonempty positive-step integer range, or <code>:</code> per dimension. Integer dimensions are dropped. Vectors additionally support integer index vectors and Boolean masks; repeated indices accumulate their cotangents. Empty ranges and negative steps are explicitly rejected. General linear indexing of matrices, Cartesian index arrays, <code>view</code>, <code>setindex!</code>, and arbitrary mixed advanced indexing are not exposed. <code>tril</code>, <code>triu</code>, and nonempty <code>diag</code> use Julia&#39;s signed diagonal offsets.</p><p>Input construction and <code>Array(tensor)</code> each copy data. Raw Julia pointers are used only during the FFI call under <code>GC.@preserve</code>; Rust copies input buffers before returning. A tensor retains its shared context, so the context variable need not remain in the caller&#39;s scope. Rust may share immutable storage for shape/view operations, but no mutable Julia alias or write-through view is returned. Mutating an input Julia array or a materialized result does not modify a tensor. Explicitly closing a context invalidates every tensor that shares it. Context accesses are serialized by a Julia lock.</p><h2 id="Linear-algebra" tabindex="-1">Linear algebra <a class="header-anchor" href="#Linear-algebra" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Linear algebra {#Linear-algebra}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Julia decomposition results are lightweight Tenferro containers. They are not subtypes of Julia&#39;s standard factorization types and do not provide all their methods or keyword options. For an <code>m × n</code> matrix and <code>k = min(m,n)</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Operation</th><th style="text-align:right;">Result contract</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>svd(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Thin <code>U: (m,k)</code>, real <code>S: (k,)</code>, <code>Vt: (k,n)</code>; <code>A ≈ U * Diagonal(S) * Vt</code>; <code>.V</code> is <code>Vt&#39;</code>; iteration gives <code>U,S,V</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>qr(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Unpivoted thin <code>Q: (m,k)</code>, <code>R: (k,n)</code>; <code>A ≈ Q*R</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lu(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>P: (m,m)</code>, <code>L: (m,k)</code>, <code>U: (k,n)</code>; <code>P*A ≈ L*U</code>; no <code>.p</code> or Julia pivot-vector contract</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cholesky(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Lower factor <code>.L</code>, conjugate transpose <code>.U</code>; <code>A ≈ L*U</code>; checks Hermitian input</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eigen(A)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>.values</code>, <code>.vectors</code>; general eigenvalues and eigenvectors are complex even for real input</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>eigen(Hermitian(A,uplo))</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Real eigenvalues; uses only the selected triangle, with real diagonal</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>A \\ B</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Square solve, or least squares for rectangular <code>A</code>; accepts vector or matrix right-hand side</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>LowerTriangular(A) \\ B</code>, <code>UpperTriangular(A) \\ B</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Uses the selected triangle</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>det</code>, <code>inv</code>, <code>pinv</code>, <code>norm</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Scalar determinant, inverse, pseudoinverse, and Euclidean/Frobenius norm</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Eigenvalues use Julia-compatible sorting by default; <code>sortby=nothing</code> retains backend order. Decomposition signs and complex phases are not fixed to match Julia; compare reconstructions, invariant quantities, or consistently aligned factors. Batch dimensions, pivoted QR, full SVD, configurable pseudoinverse tolerances, and general norm orders are not exposed. Full SVD is explicitly rejected because Rust does not supply its AD rule.</p><p>The adapter propagates numerical failures from Rust. Singular solves/inverses, nonpositive Cholesky inputs, nonconvergence, changing rank, repeated singular or eigenvalues, zero norm, and pivot changes may fail or have undefined or ill-conditioned derivatives. These are not covered by smooth-gradient claims. The default <code>pinv</code> rank threshold belongs to Rust; it is not promised to equal Julia&#39;s default threshold at a numerical rank boundary.</p>',31)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
